        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1166,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Takes the mean of each window of values: windows side by side shrink a sequence (or an image) by that factor, each value standing for the average of the ones it replaces; a window sliding one value at a time smooths a stream, a moving average.\n\nUse it to bring a fast stream down to a slower rate without the aliasing of simply dropping frames, to make a small, blurred version of an image or grid (pool over the last two dims), or to smooth a buffer of joint data over time. It is also the averaging layer inside many networks. The answer stays on the input's device.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.avg_pool <window> <step>\nDefaults: window 2, step the same as the window (windows side by side).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.avg_pool 4\nt.avg_pool 8 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values (a list or tensor). Every input is pooled and sent.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values each window holds (each side of a tile, over two dims).\n\nstep (int box, also an inlet):\nHow far each window moves on from the last. The same as the window (the default) takes windows side by side, with no overlap: the length shrinks by that factor. 1 slides the window one value at a time: one result per position - a moving average.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dim to pool along: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column - over time, for a buffer of frames x joints.\n\npool over:\nAn option, under the node. one dim (the default), or last two dims - square tiles of an image or grid, window x window, every image of a stack at once.\n\npartial window:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default), values left over at the end that do not fill a window are dropped. On, they make one last, shorter window.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npooled:\nThe mean of each window, the shape of the input with the pooled dim shortened.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 1 5 3 8 2 2 9 0 6 in windows of 3 side by side - 1 5 3, 8 2 2, 9 0 6 - gives their means, 3 4 5.\n\nBottom: the numbers 0 to 15 as a 4 x 4 image, pooled over the last two dims in 2 x 2 tiles: a 2 x 2 image, each value the mean of its tile - 2.5 4.5 10.5 12.5.\n\nRELATED:\nt.max_pool keeps the peaks instead. t.convolve smooths with any weighting;\nt.interpolate resizes to any size. t.mean along a dim averages everything at once.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Takes the mean of each window of values: windows side by side shrink a sequence (or an image) by that factor, each value standing for the average of the ones it replaces; a window sliding one value at a time smooths a stream, a moving average.\n\nUse it to bring a fast stream down to a slower rate without the aliasing of simply dropping frames, to make a small, blurred version of an image or grid (pool over the last two dims), or to smooth a buffer of joint data over time. It is also the averaging layer inside many networks. The answer stays on the input's device.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.avg_pool <window> <step>\nDefaults: window 2, step the same as the window (windows side by side).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.avg_pool 4\nt.avg_pool 8 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values (a list or tensor). Every input is pooled and sent.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values each window holds (each side of a tile, over two dims).\n\nstep (int box, also an inlet):\nHow far each window moves on from the last. The same as the window (the default) takes windows side by side, with no overlap: the length shrinks by that factor. 1 slides the window one value at a time: one result per position - a moving average.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dim to pool along: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column - over time, for a buffer of frames x joints.\n\npool over:\nAn option, under the node. one dim (the default), or last two dims - square tiles of an image or grid, window x window, every image of a stack at once.\n\npartial window:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default), values left over at the end that do not fill a window are dropped. On, they make one last, shorter window.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npooled:\nThe mean of each window, the shape of the input with the pooled dim shortened.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 1 5 3 8 2 2 9 0 6 in windows of 3 side by side - 1 5 3, 8 2 2, 9 0 6 - gives their means, 3 4 5.\n\nBottom: the numbers 0 to 15 as a 4 x 4 image, pooled over the last two dims in 2 x 2 tiles: a 2 x 2 image, each value the mean of its tile - 2.5 4.5 10.5 12.5.\n\nRELATED:\nt.max_pool keeps the peaks instead. t.convolve smooths with any weighting;\nt.interpolate resizes to any size. t.mean along a dim averages everything at once."
        },
